{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"3815f8b0-8c60-4890-93a0-2fcc4c634f88","hash":"9a39a41405a3752df707d2c380146739be6715694b8fb738b1b497410cf79c9f","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NgJebPxehFsDBvzjcxUAYa","name":"PowerShell","input":{"command":"$env:CLOUDSDK_CORE_DISABLE_PROMPTS='1'; $P='lcv-ideas-and-software'; \"=== findings SCC ativos CONTAINER_IMAGE_VULNERABILITY no projeto\"; $raw = gcloud.cmd scc findings list \"projects/$P\" --filter='state=\"ACTIVE\" AND category=\"CONTAINER_IMAGE_VULNERABILITY\"' --format=json --page-size=500 2>&1; try { $f = $raw | ConvertFrom-Json } catch { \"erro: $raw\"; $f=@() }; \"total: $(@($f).Count)\"; $f | ForEach-Object { \"{0} | mute={1} | {2} | {3}\" -f $_.finding.severity, $_.finding.mute, ($_.finding.resourceName -replace '.*/',''), $_.finding.eventTime } | Sort-Object -Unique; \"=== digests existentes\"; $repos = gcloud.cmd artifacts repositories list --project $P --format='csv[no-heading](name,location)' 2>$null; foreach ($line in $repos) { $name,$loc = $line -split ','; $imgs = gcloud.cmd artifacts docker images list \"$loc-docker.pkg.dev/$P/$name\" --include-tags --format='csv[no-heading](package,version,tags)' 2>$null; \"repo $name ($loc): $(@($imgs).Count) imagens\"; $imgs | ForEach-Object { \"  \" + ($_ -replace \"$loc-docker.pkg.dev/$P/$name/\",'') } }","description":"Lista os findings SCC ativos do projeto e todos os digests existentes no Artifact Registry","timeout":300000},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011aU2KvEkGCYuoxbgU3heD8","name":"PowerShell","input":{"command":"$env:CLOUDSDK_CORE_DISABLE_PROMPTS='1'; $P='lcv-ideas-and-software'; foreach ($img in 'us-central1-docker.pkg.dev/lcv-ideas-and-software/astrologo-paid-cmek/astrologo-paid@sha256:f75b22018296fade335cd423f88dbd34639616e927996354b9aebb2b485bf9e0','us-central1-docker.pkg.dev/lcv-ideas-and-software/astrologo-paid-cmek/astrologo-paid-runner@sha256:9458017e49fd2d3633acb38b663b52bbeee89ec551d7b9c82681431c6e4cc68d') { $d = gcloud.cmd artifacts docker images describe $img --show-package-vulnerability --format=json --project $P 2>&1 | Out-String; try { $j = $d | ConvertFrom-Json; $v = $j.package_vulnerability_summary; \"$($img -replace '.*/','') | scan=$($v.vulnerabilities.PSObject.Properties.Count) grupos | status=$($j.discovery_summary.discovery[0].discovery.analysisStatus) | atualizado=$($j.discovery_summary.discovery[0].updateTime)\"; if ($v.vulnerabilities) { $v.vulnerabilities.PSObject.Properties | ForEach-Object { \"   $($_.Name): $(@($_.Value).Count)\" } } else { '   sem vulnerabilidades listadas' } } catch { \"$img -> $($d.Substring(0,[Math]::Min(300,$d.Length)))\" } }; \"agora: $(Get-Date -Format 'dd/MM/yyyy HH:mm:ss') (Brasília)\"","description":"Consulta a análise de vulnerabilidades do Artifact Analysis das duas imagens que servem agora","timeout":300000},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GtUsD5DKyt76ujErTT6UgF","name":"mcp__google-dev-docs__search_documents","input":{"query":"Google Play Developer API data safety form content rating questionnaire target audience app category available via API"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XPjZshNuutBSAsvuvyBN62","name":"mcp__google-dev-docs__search_documents","input":{"query":"Play Billing test purchases license testers requirements internal testing track Google account on device"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"3815f8b0-8c60-4890-93a0-2fcc4c634f88","hash":"439660a030e2f0c138792da6fce83bdde49d324d1934af883b974341ef6ee7a8","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","content":"Exit code 2\n=== findings SCC ativos CONTAINER_IMAGE_VULNERABILITY no projeto\r\nerro: ERROR: (gcloud.scc.findings.list) INVALID_ARGUMENT: Invalid Filter. Filter must be non-null and filter upon a field in the request. Example: \"event_time = 123 OR event_time >= 123 OR event_time <= 123\" []\r\ntotal: 0\r\n=== digests existentes\r\nrepo astrologo-paid-cmek (): 0 imagens","is_error":true,"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NgJebPxehFsDBvzjcxUAYa"},{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011aU2KvEkGCYuoxbgU3heD8","type":"tool_result","content":"astrologo-paid@sha256:f75b22018296fade335cd423f88dbd34639616e927996354b9aebb2b485bf9e0 | scan=0 grupos | status=FINISHED_SUCCESS | atualizado=10/30/2026 02:57:31\r\n   sem vulnerabilidades listadas\r\nastrologo-paid-runner@sha256:9458017e49fd2d3633acb38b663b52bbeee89ec551d7b9c82681431c6e4cc68d | scan=0 grupos | status=FINISHED_SUCCESS | atualizado=10/30/2026 02:59:36\r\n   sem vulnerabilidades listadas\r\nagora: 30/10/2026 01:53:50 (Brasília)","is_error":false},{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GtUsD5DKyt76ujErTT6UgF","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"results\":[{\"content\":\"# Google Play Developer APIs\\n\\nThe Google Play Developer API allows you to perform a number of publishing and app-management tasks. It includes two components:\\n\\n* The\\n[Subscriptions and In-App Purchases API](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher#subscriptions)lets you manage in-app purchases and subscriptions.\\n* The\\n[Publishing API](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher#publishing)lets you upload and publish apps, and perform other publishing-related tasks.\\n\\n\\n## Subscriptions and In-App Purchases\\n\\nYou can use the Google Play Developer API to manage:\\n\\n* Catalog management\\n\\n    * [One Time Products](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest/v3/monetization.onetimeproducts)\\n    * [In-App Products](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest/v3/inappproducts)\\n    * [Subscription Products](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest/v3/monetization.subscriptions)\\n\\n\\n* [Products purchase status](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest/v3/purchases.products)\\n* [Subscriptions purchase management](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest/v3/purchases.subscriptions)\\n\\nYou can use these resources to check the status of purchases, and to modify and cancel recurring purchases. To learn more about how to sell subscriptions in an Android app, read\\n[Google Play In-app Billing](https://developer.android.com/google/play/billing/index.html)on the Android Developers site.\\n\\n## Publishing API\\n\\nThe Google Play Developer Publishing API allows you to automate frequent tasks having to do with app production and distribution. This provides functions similar to those available to a developer through the\\n[Play Console](https://play.google.com/console), such as:\\n\\n* Uploading new versions of an app\\n* Releasing apps, by assigning APKs to various\\n[Tracks](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/tracks)(alpha, beta, staged rollout, or production)\\n* Creating and modifying Google Play Store listings, including localized text and graphics and multi-device screenshots\\n\\nThose tasks are performed using the new\\n[edits](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/edits)functionality, which take a transactional approach to making changes; you bundle several changes into a single draft edit , then commit the changes all at once. (None of the changes take effect until the edit is committed.)\\n\\n## Getting Started\\n\\nTo start using the Google Play Developer API, have a look at the\\n[Getting Started](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/getting_started)page. There are Java and Python libraries you can use to manage interaction with the Publishing API. We have provided code samples on the\\n[Client Libraries and Code Samples](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/libraries)page. If you are using other languages, you can access the REST APIs directly via HTTP. If you’re having issues using the Google Play Developer API,\\n[we’re here to help](https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/7218994).\\n\\n## Other APIs\\n\\n* The\\n[Google Play Games Services Publishing API](https://developer.android.com/games/pgs/publishing/publishing)allows you to automate frequent tasks having to do with game services production and distribution.\\n* The\\n[Reporting API](https://developer.android.com/google/play/developer-api#reporting)lets you retrieve information about your app's quality from Android vitals.\\n* The\\n[Reply to Reviews API](https://developer.android.com/google/play/developer-api#reviews)lets you retrieve and reply to reviews of your app.\\n* The\\n[Permissions API](https://developer.android.com/google/play/developer-api#permissions)lets you automate permission management within the Play Console.\\n* The\\n[Play Games Services Management API](https://developer.android.com/games/pgs/management/management)lets you issue REST calls to programmatically control the metadata underlying the Google Play Games Services features.\\n* The\\n[Voided Purchases API](https://developer.android.com/google/play/developer-api#voided-purchases)provides a list of orders that are associated with purchases that a user has voided.\\n\\n\",\"id\":\"c1\",\"parent\":\"documents/developers.google.com/android-publisher\"},{\"content\":\"## 7. Target Audience & Content (TAC) Declaration\\n\\nDeclaration ID: POLICY_DECLARATION_ID_TARGET_AUDIENCE_CONTENT\\n\\n* Question ID: POLICY_QUESTION_ID_TAC_TARGET_AGE_GROUPS\\n\\n    * Type: Multiple Choice\\n    * Question Text: What are the target age groups of the app?\\n    * Choices:\\n\\n        * 5 and under ( POLICY_RESPONSE_CHOICE_ID_TAC_AGE_FIVE_AND_UNDER )\\n        * 6-8 ( POLICY_RESPONSE_CHOICE_ID_TAC_AGE_SIX_TO_EIGHT )\\n        * 9-12 ( POLICY_RESPONSE_CHOICE_ID_TAC_AGE_NINE_TO_TWELVE )\\n        * 13-15 ( POLICY_RESPONSE_CHOICE_ID_TAC_AGE_THIRTEEN_TO_FIFTEEN )\\n        * 16-17 ( POLICY_RESPONSE_CHOICE_ID_TAC_AGE_SIXTEEN_AND_SEVENTEEN )\\n        * 18 and over ( POLICY_RESPONSE_CHOICE_ID_TAC_AGE_EIGHTEEN_AND_ABOVE )\\n\\n\\n    * Conditions: Always required. A consecutive set of age bands must be selected.\\n\\n\\n* Question ID: POLICY_QUESTION_ID_TAC_RESTRICT_MINOR_ACCESS\\n\\n    * Type: Multiple Choice\\n    * Question Text: Restrict users that Google has determined to be minors from the app (optional)\\n    * Helper Text: The developer confirms that Google Play should block users determined to be minors from: Searching for or downloading the app on Google Play; Making in-app purchases, including new subscription sign ups, and renewals for existing subscribers. I acknowledge that Google Play may not be able to block all minor users who have not declared themselves to be under the age of 18.\\n    * Choices:\\n\\n        * POLICY_RESPONSE_CHOICE_ID_TAC_RESTRICT_MINOR_ACCESS\\n\\n\\n    * Conditions: Shown if only POLICY_RESPONSE_CHOICE_ID_TAC_AGE_EIGHTEEN_AND_ABOVE is selected.\\n\\n\\n* Question ID: POLICY_QUESTION_ID_TAC_COMPLIES_WITH_CHILDREN_LAWS_CERTIFICATION\\n\\n    * Type: Multiple Choice\\n    * Question Text: The developer certifies that the app (including all APIs, SDKs, and ads) complies with all applicable laws and regulations relating to children. Examples include the US Children's Online Privacy Protection Act COPPA, and the EU General Data Protection Regulation GDPR.\\n    * Choices:\\n\\n        * POLICY_RESPONSE_CHOICE_ID_TAC_CHILDREN_LAWS_CERTIFIED\\n\\n\\n    * Conditions: Required if any age group 12 and under is selected.\\n\\n\\n* Question ID: POLICY_QUESTION_ID_TAC_ADS_FROM_GOOGLE_PLAY_CERTIFIED_AD_NETWORKS\\n\\n    * Type: Multiple Choice\\n    * Question Text: The developer certifies that all ads in the app are from Google Play certified ad networks and are suitable for all audiences, including families and children.\\n    * Choices:\\n\\n        * POLICY_RESPONSE_CHOICE_ID_TAC_ADS_FROM_GOOGLE_PLAY_CERTIFIED_AD_NETWORKS\\n\\n\\n    * Conditions: Required if any age group 12 and under is selected AND the app has declared it contains ads in the\\n[Ads Declaration](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/app-store-review/policy-declarations#ads-declaration).\\n\\n\\n* Question ID: POLICY_QUESTION_ID_TAC_JOIN_TEACHER_APPROVED_PROGRAM\\n\\n    * Type: Single Choice\\n    * Question Text: Join the Teacher Approved program\\n    * Choices:\\n\\n        * Include the app in the Teacher Approved program ( POLICY_RESPONSE_CHOICE_ID_TAC_INCLUDE_IN_TEACHER_APPROVED )\\n        * Do not include the app in the Teacher Approved program ( POLICY_RESPONSE_CHOICE_ID_TAC_DO_NOT_INCLUDE_IN_TEACHER_APPROVED )\\n\\n\\n    * Conditions: Required if any age group 12 and under is selected.\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n### Example JSON\\n\\n{ \\\"declarationId\\\": \\\"POLICY_DECLARATION_ID_TARGET_AUDIENCE_CONTENT\\\", \\\"responses\\\": [ { \\\"questionId\\\": \\\"POLICY_QUESTION_ID_TAC_TARGET_AGE_GROUPS\\\", \\\"multipleChoiceResponse\\\": { \\\"values\\\": [ \\\"POLICY_RESPONSE_CHOICE_ID_TAC_AGE_THIRTEEN_TO_FIFTEEN\\\", \\\"POLICY_RESPONSE_CHOICE_ID_TAC_AGE_SIXTEEN_AND_SEVENTEEN\\\", \\\"POLICY_RESPONSE_CHOICE_ID_TAC_AGE_EIGHTEEN_AND_ABOVE\\\" ] } } ] }\\n\\n## 8. Ads Declaration\\n\\nDeclaration ID: POLICY_DECLARATION_ID_ADS_DECLARATION\\n\\n* Question ID: POLICY_QUESTION_ID_ADS_CONTAINS_ADS\\n\\n    * Type: Single Choice\\n    * Question Text: Does the app contain ads? Check the\\n[Ads policy](https://play.google.com/about/monetization-ads/ads/)to make sure the app is compliant.\\n    * Allowed Responses:\\n\\n        * Yes, the app contains ads ( POLICY_RESPONSE_CHOICE_ID_ADS_HAS_ADS )\\n        * No, the app does not contain ads ( POLICY_RESPONSE_CHOICE_ID_ADS_NO_ADS )\\n\\n\\n    * Conditions: Always required.\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n### Example JSON\\n\\n{ \\\"declarationId\\\": \\\"POLICY_DECLARATION_ID_ADS_DECLARATION\\\", \\\"responses\\\": [ { \\\"questionId\\\": \\\"POLICY_QUESTION_ID_ADS_CONTAINS_ADS\\\", \\\"singleChoiceResponse\\\": { \\\"value\\\": \\\"POLICY_RESPONSE_CHOICE_ID_ADS_HAS_ADS\\\" } } ] }\",\"id\":\"c10\",\"parent\":\"documents/developers.google.com/android-publisher/app-store-review/policy-declarations\"},{\"content\":\"Lets Android application developers access their Google Play accounts. At a high level, the expected workflow is to \\\"insert\\\" an Edit, make changes as necessary, and then \\\"commit\\\" it.\\n\\n* [REST Resource: v3.applications](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest#v3.applications)\\n* [REST Resource: v3.applications.deviceTierConfigs](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest#v3.applications.deviceTierConfigs)\\n* [REST Resource: v3.applications.tracks.releases](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest#v3.applications.tracks.releases)\\n* [REST Resource: v3.apprecovery](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest#v3.apprecovery)\\n* [REST Resource: v3.appsigning](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest#v3.appsigning)\\n* [REST Resource: v3.appstorecatalog.recentAppViews](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest#v3.appstorecatalog.recentAppViews)\\n* [REST Resource: v3.appstorecatalog.recentUpdateEvents](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest#v3.appstorecatalog.recentUpdateEvents)\\n* [REST Resource: v3.edits](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest#v3.edits)\\n* [REST Resource: v3.edits.apks](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest#v3.edits.apks)\\n* [REST Resource: v3.edits.bundles](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest#v3.edits.bundles)\\n* [REST Resource: v3.edits.countryavailability](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest#v3.edits.countryavailability)\\n* [REST Resource: v3.edits.deobfuscationfiles](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest#v3.edits.deobfuscationfiles)\\n* [REST Resource: v3.edits.details](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest#v3.edits.details)\\n* [REST Resource: v3.edits.expansionfiles](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest#v3.edits.expansionfiles)\\n* [REST Resource: v3.edits.images](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest#v3.edits.images)\\n* [REST Resource: v3.edits.listings](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest#v3.edits.listings)\\n* [REST Resource: v3.edits.testers](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest#v3.edits.testers)\\n* [REST Resource: v3.edits.tracks](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest#v3.edits.tracks)\\n* [REST Resource: v3.externaltransactions](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest#v3.externaltransactions)\\n* [REST Resource: v3.generatedapks](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest#v3.generatedapks)\\n* [REST Resource: v3.grants](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest#v3.grants)\\n* [REST Resource: v3.inappproducts](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest#v3.inappproducts)\\n* [REST Resource: v3.internalappsharingartifacts](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest#v3.internalappsharingartifacts)\\n* [REST Resource: v3.monetization](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest#v3.monetization)\\n* [REST Resource: v3.monetization.onetimeproducts](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest#v3.monetization.onetimeproducts)\\n* [REST Resource: v3.monetization.onetimeproducts.purchaseOptions](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest#v3.monetization.onetimeproducts.purchaseOptions)\\n* [REST Resource: v3.monetization.onetimeproducts.purchaseOptions.offers](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest#v3.monetization.onetimeproducts.purchaseOptions.offers)\\n* [REST Resource: v3.monetization.subscriptions](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest#v3.monetization.subscriptions)\\n* [REST Resource: v3.monetization.subscriptions.basePlans](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest#v3.monetization.subscriptions.basePlans)\\n* [REST Resource: v3.monetization.subscriptions.basePlans.offers](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest#v3.monetization.subscriptions.basePlans.offers)\\n* [REST Resource: v3.orders](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest#v3.orders)\\n* [REST Resource: v3.purchases.products](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest#v3.purchases.products)\\n* [REST Resource: v3.purchases.productsv2](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest#v3.purchases.productsv2)\\n* [REST Resource: v3.purchases.subscriptions](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest#v3.purchases.subscriptions)\\n* [REST Resource: v3.purchases.subscriptionsv2](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest#v3.purchases.subscriptionsv2)\\n* [REST Resource: v3.purchases.voidedpurchases](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest#v3.purchases.voidedpurchases)\\n* [REST Resource: v3.reviews](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest#v3.reviews)\\n* [REST Resource: v3.systemapks.variants](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest#v3.systemapks.variants)\\n* [REST Resource: v3.users](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest#v3.users)\\n\\n\\n## Service: androidpublisher.googleapis.com\\n\\nTo call this service, we recommend that you use the Google-provided\\n[client libraries](https://cloud.google.com/apis/docs/client-libraries-explained). If your application needs to use your own libraries to call this service, use the following information when you make the API requests.\\n\\n### Discovery document\\n\\nA\\n[Discovery Document](https://developers.google.com/discovery/v1/reference/apis)is a machine-readable specification for describing and consuming REST APIs. It is used to build client libraries, IDE plugins, and other tools that interact with Google APIs. One service may provide multiple discovery documents. This service provides the following discovery document:\\n\\n* [https://androidpublisher.googleapis.com/$discovery/rest?version=v3](https://androidpublisher.googleapis.com/$discovery/rest?version=v3)\\n\\n\\n\\n### Service endpoint\\n\\nA\\n[service endpoint](https://cloud.google.com/apis/design/glossary#api_service_endpoint)is a base URL that specifies the network address of an API service. One service might have multiple service endpoints. This service has the following service endpoint and all URIs below are relative to this service endpoint:\\n\\n* https://androidpublisher.googleapis.com\\n\\n\\n\\n## REST Resource: v3.applications\\n\\n_START_OF_TABLE_\\nTABLE_IN_MARKDOWN:\\n| Methods |  |\\n|-|-|\\n| [dataSafety](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest/v3/applications/dataSafety) | POST /androidpublisher/v3/applications/{packageName}/dataSafety Writes the Safety Labels declaration of an app. |\\n\\n_END_OF_TABLE_\\n\\n\\n## REST Resource: v3.applications.deviceTierConfigs\\n\\n_START_OF_TABLE_\\nTABLE_IN_MARKDOWN:\\n| Methods |  |\\n|-|-|\\n| [create](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest/v3/applications.deviceTierConfigs/create) | POST /androidpublisher/v3/applications/{packageName}/deviceTierConfigs Creates a new device tier config for an app. |\\n| [get](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest/v3/applications.deviceTierConfigs/get) | GET /androidpublisher/v3/applications/{packageName}/deviceTierConfigs/{deviceTierConfigId} Returns a particular device tier config. |\\n| [list](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest/v3/applications.deviceTierConfigs/list) | GET /androidpublisher/v3/applications/{packageName}/deviceTierConfigs Returns created device tier configs, ordered by descending creation time. |\\n\\n_END_OF_TABLE_\\n\\n\\n## REST Resource: v3.applications.tracks.releases\\n\\n_START_OF_TABLE_\\nTABLE_IN_MARKDOWN:\\n| Methods |  |\\n|-|-|\\n| [list](https://developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest/v3/applications.tracks.releases/list) | GET /androidpublisher/v3/{parent=applications/*/tracks/*}/releases Returns the list of all releases for a given track. |\\n\\n_END_OF_TABLE_\\n\",\"id\":\"c1\",\"parent\":\"documents/developers.google.com/android-publisher/api-ref/rest\"},{\"content\":\"Note: This page supplements the guidance on how to\\n[provide information for Google Play's Data safety section](https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/10787469)when you publish an app to Google Play. It's recommended that you read the Help Center article before you review this page. The Play Console includes a Data safety form on the App content page. In this form, you explain to users which types of user data your app collects and shares. This information helps promote data transparency and user trust. To help you fill out this form, this guide provides examples of places in your app's code where your app may collect different types of user data. In particular, it provides examples of permission declarations and API calls that your app may use to collect or share a particular type of user data, which would require you to declare that collection or sharing in the Data safety form. The guide has the following format:\\n\\n* The main headings list the different categories of data types that are available in the Data safety form, along with a non-exhaustive list of ways that your app, or a library included in your app, may access user data related to that category.\\n* The sub-headings list the different data types that are available in the form, to remind you of the data types that are part of a particular category.\\n\\nWhile we are offering this guidance to make it easier for you to complete the Data safety form, you alone are responsible for making complete and accurate declarations in your app's Play store listing. Only you possess all the information required to complete the Data safety form. Google cannot make determinations on behalf of developers regarding how they collect or handle user data based on their particular usage and practices. It's up to developers to appropriately handle any user data that their apps collect or share. When we become aware of a discrepancy between your app behavior and your declaration, we may take appropriate action, including enforcement action. The details in this guidance are subject to change as we continue working with developers and to improve both developer and user experiences. For more information, read this\\n[blog post](https://android-developers.googleblog.com/2021/10/launching-data-safety-in-play-console.html). Note: Your app may include third-party SDKs and libraries that can access user data. If a third-party SDK or library in your app collects or shares user data, you must reflect this collection and sharing in the Data safety form.\",\"id\":\"c1\",\"parent\":\"documents/developer.android.com/privacy-and-security/declare-data-use\"},{\"content\":\"### Build age-appropriate experiences on Google Play\\n\\nDeliver safe, age-appropriate experiences in your apps and games Google Play gives you a suite of tools to help you simplify building age-appropriate experiences. By combining Google Play Console controls and our API, you can dynamically tailor your in-app content and features to the right age groups—without the complexity of collecting sensitive user data like exact birthdate or age yourself.\\n\\n### Content restrictions\\n\\nGoogle Play may restrict downloads by users identified as minors based on the age rating assigned by IARC through parental controls and, in some regions, apply restrictions at the Google Play store level. Google Play level restrictions\\n[Complete](https://play.google.com/console/developers/app/app-content/content-rating-overview)the mandatory International Age Rating Coalition (IARC) questionnaire in Play Console, to automatically receive ratings across regional rating authorities such as PEGI, ESRB, ClassInd and more. In some regions, Google Play restricts users identified as minors from downloading or purchasing mature content, such as apps rated 18+.\\n[Learn more about content ratings](https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/9859655#How_content_ratings_are_used)Parental controls Parents can use\\n[Google Family Link](https://families.google/familylink/)to set content filters based on maturity rating level. On supervised devices, Google Play uses your app's rating to limit what supervised users can discover and download.\\n[Learn more about parental controls](https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/1075738)\\n\\n### Restrict minor access\\n\\nLimit app discovery and distribution to your age appropriate audience, directly from Google Play Console. When you enable Restrict Minor Access, users who are identified as minors can't discover, download, or purchase your app. This setting applies globally to protect younger audiences. While Restrict Minor Access is required for apps in designated\\n[age-restricted content and functionality policy](https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/16302250)categories, any developer can proactively opt in by using Play Console to help direct their content only to adults.\\n\\n### Play Age Signals API\\n\\nDynamically adapt your app experiences based on user age ranges. The Play Age Signals API lets your app request a user's age range. By default, the API returns age ranges of 0–12, 13–15, 16–17, and 18+. You can also set\\n[custom age ranges](https://play.google.com/console/developers/app/age-signals)in Play Console to get age ranges that suit your app content. Step 1\\n\\n### Keep IARC content ratings up to date [Required]\\n\\nEnsure your responses to the International Age Rating Coalition (IARC) questionnaire matches every new version of your app in the Play Console. This keeps your regional ratings accurate and facilitates Family Link appropriately restricting minors' access to your app. Step 2\\n\\n### Check regional protections on Play\\n\\nReview the age-related Google Play tools and features that apply in the countries and regions where you distribute your app. Step 3\\n\\n### Determine if Restrict Minor Access is mandatory for your app [Required]\\n\\nDepending on your app category, you may need to enable Restrict Minor Access to comply with\\n[Google Play policy](https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/16302250). You can easily configure this setting in the Play Console to restrict discovery, downloads, and in-app purchases and new subscriptions by users identified as minors. Step 4\",\"id\":\"c1\",\"parent\":\"documents/developer.android.com/distribute/age-appropriate-experiences\"}]}"},{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XPjZshNuutBSAsvuvyBN62","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"results\":[{\"content\":\"## License Testers\\n\\nTo configure license testers, see\\n[Test in-app billing with application licensing](https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/6062777). Using license testers provide the following benefits:\\n\\n* Ordinarily, the Google Play Billing Library is blocked for apps that aren't signed and uploaded to Google Play. License testers can bypass this check, meaning you can sideload apps for testing, even for apps using debug builds with debug signatures without the need to upload to the new version of your app. Note that the package name must match that of the app that is configured for Google Play, and the Google Account must be a license tester for the Google Play Console account.\\n* License testers have access to test payment methods that avoid charging the testers real money for purchases. You can also use test payment methods to simulate certain situations, such as when a payment is declined. Figure 1 shows these test forms of payment as they appear within the purchase flow.\\n* License testers can\\n[rapidly test subscription features](https://developer.android.com/google/play/billing/test#subs).\\n\\nHere are some additional details about the test purchase process:\\n\\n* Test purchases use the same app purchase flow used by actual purchases.\\n* Taxes are not computed for test purchases.\\n* Google Play indicates a test purchase by displaying a notice across the center of the purchase dialog.\\n\\nYou can confirm the account that is making a purchase by expanding the purchase dialog. Note the following:\\n\\n* Test accounts must be on the tester's Android-powered device.\\n* If the device has more than one account, the purchase is made with the account that downloaded the app.\\n* If none of the accounts have downloaded the app, the purchase is made with the first account.\\n\\nUse Google Play\\n[test tracks](https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/3131213)for pre-distribution validation, such as allowing your QA team to qualify new releases. With test tracks, license testers can install and evaluate unreleased versions of your app from Google Play. Regular users can make real purchases using standard payment methods. Note: Users incur actual charges for their test track purchases unless the user is a license tester. Draft apps and internal test track releases are subject to\\n[spend limits](https://developer.android.com/google/play/billing/test#spend-limits). To test your Google Play Billing Library integration using test tracks, do the following:\\n\\n* Publish your app to a\\n[test track](https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/3131213). Note that after you publish an app to a testing track, it can take a few hours for the app to be available for testers.\\n* Ensure each tester\\n[opts-in to your app's test](https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/3131213). On your test's opt-in URL, your testers see an explanation of what it means to be a tester along with a link to opt-in.\\n\\nYou can test your integration on any Android-powered hardware device running Android 1.6 or higher. The most current version of the Google Play application must be installed on the device. For general information about how to set up a device for use in developing Android applications, see\\n[Using Hardware Devices](https://developer.android.com/tools/device). Note:\",\"id\":\"c2\",\"parent\":\"documents/developer.android.com/google/play/billing/test\"},{\"content\":\"## Test promo codes\\n\\nYou can use the Google Play Console to\\n[create codes for your own testing](https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/6321495). Keep in mind that you may only create 500 promo codes per quarter across all managed products in an app. You should test the following promo code redemption scenarios:\\n\\n* When the promo code is entered in the purchase dialog that was launched within your app.\\n* When the promo code is redeemed in the Google Play Store app.\\n* When the promo code is redeemed at\\n[https://play.google.com/store](https://play.google.com/store)using the Redeem button in the left-hand navigation.\\n\\nWithin these scenarios, you should test redeeming codes in as many ways as possible. Perform the following tests at a minimum:\\n\\n* Redemption before the app is installed.\\n* Redemption while the app is running in the foreground. Note that for this test, you need another device to test using the Google Play Store app. Be sure to test redemptions from different screens in your app.\\n* Redemption with\\n[multi-window mode](https://developer.android.com/guide/topics/ui/multi-window), where both your app and the Google Play Store app are being displayed at the same time.\\n\\nFor each test, make sure that the item is correctly detected and that the user is notified.\\n\\n## Test the purchase experience in different regions\\n\\nYou can test the purchase experience in different regions with the\\n[Play Billing Lab](https://developer.android.com/google/play/billing/test#play-billing-lab)app. To use the Play Billing Lab, you must be a license tester. Use the following steps to test:\\n\\n* Register the app billing user as a license tester.\\n* Log into Play Billing Lab app with the same user.\\n* Select the required country and apply the change in Play Billing Lab.\\n* Launch the purchase flow in the app under testing.\\n\\n[Play Billing Lab](https://developer.android.com/google/play/billing/test#play-billing-lab)lets you test offer regional eligibility and the user experience in any region, regardless of where you are physically testing. Note: Gmail accounts created for testing purposes shouldn't be used to make purchases using real instruments on Google Play. This includes other apps and purchases such as Google Books.\\n\\n## Test the purchase experience using real payment methods\\n\\nYou can test the purchase experience with real payment methods with the\\n[Play Billing Lab](https://developer.android.com/google/play/billing/test#play-billing-lab)Android app. Note:\\n\\n* You need permissions to access this feature. Contact your Google Play Business Development manager to opt in.\\n* You lose all other license tester features and Play Billing Lab configurations when you enable testing for real payment methods. Request a\\n[refund](https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/2741495)for each purchase, or you will be charged a\\n[service fee](https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/11131145).\\n\\nUse the following steps to test real payment methods:\\n\\n* Register the Google Account user as a license tester.\\n* Log into the Play Billing Lab app with the same user.\\n* Enable real payment methods in the Play Billing Lab app.\\n* Restart and launch the purchase flow in the app under testing.\\n\\n\",\"id\":\"c12\",\"parent\":\"documents/developer.android.com/google/play/billing/test\"},{\"content\":\"\\n* While license testers are recommended for development and testing, ensure you also test your app using non-license tester accounts, either occasionally or when making large changes. Non-license testing helps to ensure that your app does not rely on testing specific logic such as renewal durations.\\n* Users in the testing tracks can also be license testers for your app.\\n\\n\\n## Play Billing Lab\\n\\nPlay Billing Lab is an Android app that helps developers test their integration with Google Play's billing system. It provides a convenient way for developers to test billing features, integrate faster, and launch with higher confidence. You can download and install Play Billing Lab from the\\n[Play Store](https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.google.android.apps.play.billingtestcompanion). Play Billing Lab lets you to do the following in your testing:\\n\\n* Change\\n[Play Country](https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/7431675)from within Play Billing Lab and apply the settings to your test. This enables\\n[testing custom user-experiences in different countries/regions](https://developer.android.com/google/play/billing/test#regions)regardless of where the tester is physically testing\\n* [Test trial or introductory offers](https://developer.android.com/google/play/billing/test#trial-offers)repeatedly with the same account\\n* [Test subscription price changes](https://developer.android.com/google/play/billing/test#price-changes)without affecting other active subscribers\\n* [Accelerate subscription state transition](https://developer.android.com/google/play/billing/test#subscription-state-transition)to accelerate subscription renewals, or move your test subscription into grace period or account hold state with one click to speed up your testing\\n* [Test with real payment methods](https://developer.android.com/google/play/billing/test#real-fop-testing)to bypass certain purchase flow risk signals\\n* [Test out-of-app purchases](https://developer.android.com/google/play/billing/test#out-of-app)to make sure that your backend can handle them\\n\\nNote:\\n\\n* Be sure to log into Play Billing Lab using the same account as the billing account. Be sure that the account is registered as a license tester for the app under test. Play country, testing trial or introductory offers, and testing with real payment methods configurations expire in 2 hours.\\n* If you get errors when applying changes in Play Billing Lab, log into Play Billing Lab again and retry. If you notice long delay for the configuration to be applied or you are using another Android-powered device to perform the testing, clear the Play Store cache manually on the device on which the test was performed after you have made configuration changes in the Play Billing Lab.\\n\\n\",\"id\":\"c3\",\"parent\":\"documents/developer.android.com/google/play/billing/test\"},{\"content\":\"## Test one-time products\\n\\n### Test consumable products\\n\\nWhen testing consumable products, test a variety of situations, including the following:\\n\\n* A successful purchase where the user receives an item. With a license tester, you can use the Test instrument, always approves payment method.\\n* A purchase where the payment method failed to be charged, and the user shouldn't receive the item. With a license tester you can use the Test instrument, always declines payment method.\\n* Ensure items can be purchased multiple times.\\n\\nVerify that purchases are properly acknowledged as described in\\n[processing purchases](https://developer.android.com/google/play/billing/integrate#process). For purchases from license testers, a purchase will be refunded after 3 minutes if your app does not acknowledge the purchase and you will receive an email about the cancellation. You can also check the Orders tab in the Google Play Console to see if an order was refunded after 3 minutes.\\n\\n### Test non-consumable products\\n\\nNon-consumables should be tested the same as consumables, but you should verify an item cannot be purchased again within your app. Be sure to verify purchase acknowledgement for both non-consumables and consumables (when applicable) since the logic to process each the two types of purchases vary. Note: To perform multiple test purchases for the same non-consumable product, you can\\n[refund and revoke purchases using Google Play Console](https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/2741495).\\n\\n### Test pending purchases\\n\\nTest a pending purchase where the item should be granted when the purchase state becomes PURCHASED . License testers have access to two test instruments for delayed forms of payment where the payment automatically completes or cancels after a couple of minutes.\\n\\n* Make a purchase with a delayed form of payment Slow test card, declines after a few minutes , as shown in figure 3. Restart the app, validate that the purchase has not been granted.\\n* Make a purchase with a delayed form of payment Slow test card, approves after a few minutes , as shown in figure 4. Wait a few minutes, validate that the purchase has been granted.\\n\\nYou can find more information at\\n[Handling pending transactions](https://developer.android.com/google/play/billing/integrate#pending).\\n\\n### Test user-initiated chargebacks\\n\\nLicense testers have access to a test instrument that lets them simulate a user-initiated chargeback.\\n\\n* Make a purchase with the test instrument Test card, approves then charges back , as shown in figure 5. Soon afterwards, your system should be made aware of the test purchase being delivered and charged back through applicable surfaces. For example, you should receive a\\n[PendingRefundReviewNotification](https://developer.android.com/google/play/billing/rtdn-reference#pending-refund-review)moments after making the purchase.\\n\\n\",\"id\":\"c4\",\"parent\":\"documents/developer.android.com/google/play/billing/test\"},{\"content\":\"## Test subscription-specific features\\n\\nThe purchase flows for one-time products and subscriptions are similar, but subscriptions have additional scenarios, such as successful or declined subscription renewals. To test renewals, you can use the Test card, always approves and Test card, always declines payment methods that are available for license testers, as shown in figure 1. Use these payment instruments to test scenarios beyond the successful subscription scenario. Caution: The resubscribe and pause features are always enabled in license tester accounts even if they're disabled in the Play Console. Test these features in license testers, confirm that they work, and then enable them in the console for all users. To test your app's behavior when these features are disabled, you must use a non-license-tester account. Similar to one-time products, verify that purchases are properly acknowledged as described in\\n[processing purchases](https://developer.android.com/google/play/billing/integrate#process). For purchases from license testers, a purchase is refunded after 3 minutes if your app does not acknowledge the purchase, and you receive an email about the cancellation. You can also check the Orders tab in Google Play Console to see if an order was refunded after 3 minutes.\\n\\n### Renewal periods\\n\\nTest subscriptions renew more quickly than actual subscriptions, and test subscriptions can renew a maximum of six times, not counting free trials and introductory periods. The following table lists the testing renewal times for subscriptions of various durations. These times are approximate. You may see small variations in the precise time of an event. To compensate for variation, call the API to view the current status after every subscription expiration date.\\n\\n_START_OF_TABLE_\\nTABLE_IN_MARKDOWN:\\n|  |  |\\n|-|-|\\n| Production subscription period | Test subscription renewal |\\n| 1 week | 5 minutes |\\n| 1 month | 5 minutes |\\n| 3 months | 10 minutes |\\n| 6 months | 15 minutes |\\n| 1 year | 30 minutes |\\n\\n_END_OF_TABLE_\\nTime-based subscription features such as free trials are also shortened for testing. The following table identifies the testing time periods associated with time-based subscription features:\\n\\n_START_OF_TABLE_\\nTABLE_IN_MARKDOWN:\\n|  |  |\\n|-|-|\\n| Feature | Test period |\\n| Account hold | 10 minutes |\\n| Free trial | 3 minutes |\\n| Grace period | 5 minutes |\\n| Introductory price period | Same as subscription test period |\\n| Pause (1 month) | 5 minutes |\\n| Pause (2 months) | 10 minutes |\\n| Pause (3 months) | 15 minutes |\\n| Price step-up consent period (applicable only for the South Korea (KR) region) | 3 minutes |\\n| Purchase acknowledgement | 5 minutes |\\n\\n_END_OF_TABLE_\\n\",\"id\":\"c5\",\"parent\":\"documents/developer.android.com/google/play/billing/test\"}]}"}]}}
