{"parentUuid":"37578f26-a92c-4a3c-9733-0587347e14c1","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"110dcd88-4da4-456d-b567-dca3461d0c76","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"toolu_01MtUyM7gwUtcS2UqcAC1cqW","content":[{"type":"tool_reference","tool_name":"mcp__github__issue_read"}]}]},"uuid":"a28ceca9-ed94-449b-890b-ad8d2dc2999e","timestamp":"2026-10-30T07:37:01.518Z","toolUseResult":{"matches":["mcp__github__issue_read"],"query":"select:mcp__github__issue_read","total_deferred_tools":849},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"37578f26-a92c-4a3c-9733-0587347e14c1","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"C:\\Users\\lcvle\\lcv-workspace","sessionId":"3815f8b0-8c60-4890-93a0-2fcc4c634f88","version":"2.1.314","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"glistening-tumbling-prism"}
{"parentUuid":"ce445ffb-dba5-4884-894e-ba3ba07af52d","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"110dcd88-4da4-456d-b567-dca3461d0c76","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Gz8qH5cZcz55k3Y6NTWNyu","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (55.2KB). Full output saved to: C:\\Users\\lcvle\\.claude\\projects\\c--Users-lcvle-lcv-workspace\\3815f8b0-8c60-4890-93a0-2fcc4c634f88\\tool-results\\toolu_01Gz8qH5cZcz55k3Y6NTWNyu.json\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\n[\n  {\n    \"type\": \"text\",\n    \"text\": \"[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"8d26616f-28d7-4210-af91-79da93ea773a\\\",\\\"title\\\":\\\"GitLab\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://linear.app/docs/gitlab\\\",\\\"snippet\\\":\\\"Linear supports linking your Linear issues to GitLab and automates your merge request workflows. Overview Link Linear issues to GitLab merge requests. Automate your MR workflow so that issues update when MRs are drafted, opened, merged and when reviews are requested. You can link single or multiple issues to a specific MR. Configure the GitLab integration We support linking GitLab merge requests for both hosted and self-hosted installations, as long as your installation is publicly available.  Navigate to Linear Settings > Features > Integrations > GitLab Click Enable to launch the set-up pop-up. Navigate to GitLab > User Settings > Personal Access Token or GitLab > Project Settings > Access Token to create a legacy API access token.\\\\n\\\\nAn access token is used to query the GitLab API for further information and also used to post issue linkbacks. Because GitLab doesn't support bot accounts, linkbacks are created under the name of the token owner. It's recommended that you create a new user for Linear to act as the bot account. When creating the legacy token, set the api or read_api scope.\\\\n\\\\nIf the read_api scope is selected, Linear will not post linkbacks to the issue on GitLab merge requests. If using a project access token, the token needs Reporter role or higher. (Optional) Enable and set a custom URL for your self-hosted GitLab installation without any path (e.g. https://gitlab.yourcompany.com). This URL needs to be accessible to public Internet and is only required for self-hosted installations. The earliest supported version of GitLab is 15.6.\\\\n\\\\nIf you need to grant access to Linear's IPs, they are documented on our Security page.\\\\n Click Connect. Linear will generate the Webhook URL after it validates the access token. Copy and paste this URL to GitLab in either: A Group's Webhook Settin\n...\n</persisted-output>"}]},"uuid":"35f2ef5a-3de4-4c5d-b7bb-449668fbdcca","timestamp":"2026-10-30T07:37:01.522Z","toolUseResult":[{"type":"text","text":"[{\"id\":\"8d26616f-28d7-4210-af91-79da93ea773a\",\"title\":\"GitLab\",\"url\":\"https://linear.app/docs/gitlab\",\"snippet\":\"Linear supports linking your Linear issues to GitLab and automates your merge request workflows. Overview Link Linear issues to GitLab merge requests. Automate your MR workflow so that issues update when MRs are drafted, opened, merged and when reviews are requested. You can link single or multiple issues to a specific MR. Configure the GitLab integration We support linking GitLab merge requests for both hosted and self-hosted installations, as long as your installation is publicly available.  Navigate to Linear Settings > Features > Integrations > GitLab Click Enable to launch the set-up pop-up. Navigate to GitLab > User Settings > Personal Access Token or GitLab > Project Settings > Access Token to create a legacy API access token.\\n\\nAn access token is used to query the GitLab API for further information and also used to post issue linkbacks. Because GitLab doesn't support bot accounts, linkbacks are created under the name of the token owner. It's recommended that you create a new user for Linear to act as the bot account. When creating the legacy token, set the api or read_api scope.\\n\\nIf the read_api scope is selected, Linear will not post linkbacks to the issue on GitLab merge requests. If using a project access token, the token needs Reporter role or higher. (Optional) Enable and set a custom URL for your self-hosted GitLab installation without any path (e.g. https://gitlab.yourcompany.com). This URL needs to be accessible to public Internet and is only required for self-hosted installations. The earliest supported version of GitLab is 15.6.\\n\\nIf you need to grant access to Linear's IPs, they are documented on our Security page.\\n Click Connect. Linear will generate the Webhook URL after it validates the access token. Copy and paste this URL to GitLab in either: A Group's Webhook Settings (For a company on GitLab's Premium or Ultimate tiers) to integrate all projects under it. A Project's Webhook Settings, to individually connect a specific project. Enable the following Triggers: Push events Comments Merge request events Pipeline events Under SSL verification, ensure Enable SSL verification is checked Click Save changes. Link Merge Requests Create a new branch Set the branch format in Linear Settings > Workspace > Integrations GitLab > Branch format. When viewing or selecting a Linear issue, use the Copy git branch name action or shortcut Cmd/Ctrl + Shift + . It will give you a branch name with the issue ID which you can use to create a new branch in GitLab. We recommend using common branch naming patterns throughout all of your teams. Add the issue ID in the MR title Include the Linear issue ID (e.g. ID-123) in the MR title when creating merge requests. Use a magic word Add a magic word + issue ID in the MR description (e.g. Fixes ENG-123). The integration cannot link MRs via comments or commit messages. The available magic words to link issues are: close, closes, closed, closing, fix, fixes, fixed, fixing, resolve, resolves, resolved, resolving, complete, completes, completed, completing, implement, implements, implemented, implementing. To link MRs to issues without them automating your issue status on MR merge, use the one of the contributing magic words: ref, references, part of, contributes to, towards, updates. When using a non-closing magic word like this, the linked MR or commit will still move the issue through other statuses per Workflow settings, but will not automate the issue's status when the MR or commit merges. Instead, it will only transition the issue through to what’s configured prior to the On MR or commit merge event. Use related to or relates to to create a reference-only link. These links do not change the issue’s status or participate in Ready for merge automation. Link multiple Linear issues to an MR To link multiple Linear issues to a single Merge Request, or to link a Merge Request after creating it, use the magic word linking method by including multiple closing statements in the MR description (e.g. Fixes ENG-123, DES-5 and ENG-256). Linking will happen after you save your changes. The Linear issue will not close until all Merge Requests have been closed/merged. Merge Request automation The merge request automation allows you to select specific statuses for your Linear issues based on MR changes—saving you the hassle of updating Linear issues manually.  Customize the MR automation in Settings > Team > Workflow. Since this is a team setting, it must be configured for each team in your workspace.\\n Ready for merge automation If you'd like to use the Merge Request automation to capture passing CI checks, please verify in GitLab that: You have enabled Pipeline events under the Webhook settings for any repo part of the GitLab integration. All pipelines that Linear should consider when determining if an MR is mergeable need to be merge request pipelines. GitLab documentation is available here. Under ‘Merge requests’ in project settings, ‘Pipelines must succeed’ is checked. If you want to capture MR approvals, please verify in Project Settings > Merge requests that Merge request approvals are required. Custom merge queues If you use a custom merge queue that merges changes and then closes the merge request, be sure to add the externally-merged label before closing it. This tells Linear to treat the merge request as successfully merged even though it was closed. Branch-specific rules You can set custom workflow automations based on particular target branches. For example, you can now configure that when a MR is merged to: staging, the issue status should change to “In QA” main, the issue status should change to “Deployed” You can also override a default rule in a particular branch with “no action” if desired, so that issues linked to a change in that branch will not change status. Branch rules can be specified using regex, e.g: ^fea/.* can set automations for all feature branches. Issue linkbacks When an issue is linked with a merge request, Linear posts a linkback message as a comment with the issue title and description. All the merge requests are also listed in the issue details in Linear. When your MR is being reviewed in GitLab, see the avatars of up to three most recent reviewers and their review states without leaving Linear. This cross-referencing makes it faster to retain context without jumping between apps. Linkbacks are managed by the scope selected. Auto-assign and update status Save yourself a few steps by toggling on our automations in Settings > My Account > Preferences > Behavior. Auto-assigns the issue to you, and/or Move the issue to the first Started status (which you can customize in Settings > Team > Workflow) when you copy the git branch name. Auto-linking GitLab supports rendering URLs pointing to Linear when you mention issue IDs in GitLab. To configure this functionality, please reference GitLab's documentation.  FAQ Can I import or sync GitLab issues? Our issue migration assistant does not support GitLab issues. We suggest customizing the CLI importer to import issues. One option is to customize the importer to support GitLab imports. Another option is to export GitLab issues to a CSV and then modify the headers and format so that it matches one of the issue trackers supported by the CLI. How do I connect a MR that is already open? Modify the MR title or description to link an issue. See the section on this page titled Linking MRs to issues. How do I remove a MR from an issue? Open the issue in Linear and right click on the merge request attachment to bring up an option to remove it. You can also do this through the command menu in Linear by viewing or selecting an issue, then searching for git. I get a notification in Linear every time a MR is open. How can I change this? Go to integration settings and remove linkbacks. This should reduce the notifications. My issue title is not showing up in GitLab comments, just the issue ID. Why? If your team is private, we won't disclose the issue title. The link will go to your Linear issue and be accessible only by users who are part of that private team. What permissions do the integrations require? On GitLab, we have both read and write access with their API (they don't have separate permissions for comments). Why does Linear need an access token? During integration setup, Linear requires an access token. The below is an exhaustive list of how that token is used: To get supplemental information about merge requests that isn't included in the webhooks we receive For posting linkbacks on linked merge requests To retrieve merge request information when linking an MR to an issue (for rich attachment) To retrieve merge request mergeability status when a pipeline completes Why are my Linear issues not progressing to my \\\"On MR ready for merge\\\" status? We currently only allow issues to transition to the status you've chosen for \\\"On MR ready for merge\\\" if you also have a status selected for \\\"On MR review request or activity\\\". Make sure that you have a status selected for this event.\\n My GitLab integration stopped working In GitLab's webhook settings, check that the webhook used for Linear is active. If it isn't, please send take a screenshot of its recent events and send it into support@linear.app for further support.  In the meantime, please click the \\\"Test\\\" button for the relevant webhook and confirm if you continue to see issues with the integration. Can I add multiple GitLab instances? It is not currently possible to connect multiple GitLab instances to your Linear workspace, you will need to select one GitLab instance to connect within your GitLab settings.\"},{\"id\":\"c84ff5e3-5c1c-4d1f-821c-2cae9d375f9f\",\"title\":\"Origin\",\"url\":\"https://linear.app/docs/origin\",\"snippet\":\"Overview Linear’s Origin integration keeps your issues and pull requests in sync. Link an Origin pull request to a Linear issue, and Linear updates the issue’s status as the PR moves from draft to review to merged, so nobody has to change statuses by hand. Every linked PR appears on its issue in Linear, with a link back to the issue from the PR in Origin. Anyone looking at either side can see where the work stands. Configure A Linear workspace owner or admin can enable the Origin integration in Settings. Once it’s enabled, members can link pull requests to issues and teams can set up workflow automations. Linking pull requests Link a PR to a Linear issue by including the issue ID in the branch name or PR title. To link more than one PR, or to link an issue after the PR already exists, use a magic word like “fixes” followed by the issue ID in the PR description: Fixes LIN-123. Linking creates a reference to the PR on the Linear issue and a link back to Linear from the PR. Status automations Workflow automations are set per team and move issues through your workflow as their PRs progress. For example, an issue can move to In Progress when a PR is opened and to Done when it’s merged. Configure each team-specific automation by navigating into the team-level Settings > Workflows & automations. Members can also turn on personal automations in their preferences, which auto-assign the issue to them and move it to In Progress when they open a linked PR.\"},{\"id\":\"ae211f07-d139-4b48-a725-bf4f408c00a0\",\"title\":\"Reviews\",\"url\":\"https://linear.app/docs/diffs\",\"snippet\":\"Overview Linear Diffs is a performant PR review tool that keeps code changes close to the issues and projects they relate to. Keeping that context together makes it easier to understand why a change was made, discuss the implementation, and follow it through review. Guided reviews further break down large PRs into a story format, so it’s easier to focus on the core changes. The Reviews sidebar section brings together pull requests that need your attention, ones you’ve authored, and reviews you’ve participated in. Read diffs, check CI results, and leave comments directly in Linear. All review activity stays in sync with GitHub, so teammates can participate from either place. Press G then R to go to Reviews, or O then R to open a specific review. Setup Organization setup To display pull request diffs and file changes inside Linear, you’ll need to grant Linear access to your repository code through the GitHub integration. If your workspace hasn’t connected GitHub yet, a Linear owner or admin can configure the GitHub integration to enable code access for the repositories you selected. If your workspace has already connected GitHub, a GitHub organization owner can enable code access for the selected repositories by updating the integration in your GitHub integration settings. Your current GitHub integration settings and any existing pull request links and history in Linear are preserved—enabling code access simply adds permissions so Linear can display diffs and file contents for the repositories you select. GitHub organizations using IP allow lists GitHub enforces your organization’s IP allow list for review actions performed as the authenticated GitHub user, even when the Linear GitHub App is installed. To allow Linear to load pull request diffs and perform review actions, add Linear’s IP addresses listed under GitHub Enterprise options → GitHub Enterprise Cloud in the GitHub integration documentation. Personal account setup Personal GitHub connections are required for accessing pull requests, repository code, and review information associated with your specific user. If your account isn’t already connected, you can do so here. If you don't see the Reviews tab in your sidebar, navigate to Settings → Code & reviews and confirm the Enable code reviews toggle is switched on. A pull request can appear in Reviews even when Linear does not have code access to its repository. To view its diff, code access must be granted for that repository. If you can’t check or change the installation’s repository access, ask your GitHub organization owner to confirm the setup. Display and grouping At the top of the Reviews tab, you can switch between two tabs: For me, which shows the pull requests you're involved in or responsible for, and Created, which displays every pull request you've authored. This makes it easy to focus on work that needs your attention while still being able to browse or reference other pull requests when needed. You can group by status, author, or repository, and sort or filter to hide draft and closed PRs. You can choose whether to display draft or closed pull requests and whether to show additional fields such as repository, failed checks, or preview links if applicable. If your workspace uses GitHub team reviewers, display options  let you control whether team-level review requests appear in your Reviews list and whether they count toward the Reviews badge, which can help reduce noise when you’re part of larger review groups. To filter pull requests by GitHub labels, open Reviews, select the filter button, and choose GitHub labels. Select the labels you want to filter by: Code review Review a pull request in Linear Linear displays all pull request activity in one place, including comments, reviews, and discussions. When you open a pull request, you can view the files changed and read through diffs directly in Linear. Line counts in pull request diffs show implementation changes by default, excluding things like tests and documentation. When this implementation count differs from the total changed lines, Linear shows a [*] indicator next to the count. Hover over it to see the full total line count. Comments appear inline alongside the relevant sections of code and you can start new threads, reply to existing ones, or react with emoji. You can complete your review directly from Linear. Approving a pull request, requesting changes, or submitting a review comment updates the review state and syncs with GitHub. Once a pull request is ready and you have permission, you can merge it directly from Linear. GitHub labels To add or remove GitHub labels, open the ••• menu at the top of a pull request and select Labels. Applied labels appear in the Labels section of the pull request’s Overview tab. You can also select the plus button beside Labels to edit them. To filter your Reviews list by label, see Display and grouping. Guides Guided reviews help you understand large pull requests faster by organizing related changes into structured sections with explanations of their purpose and impact. Guides is available on Basic, Business and Enterprise plans. Instead of working through files one-by-one to piece together what changed, Guides surface the core parts of an implementation first while grouping supporting or lower-signal changes separately. Each section pairs a high-level explanation of why a part of the change exists alongside the relevant diffs, making it easier to understand the intent behind the implementation before diving into the code itself. Guided reviews appear in a dedicated Guide tab alongside the diff view, with direct links into the relevant parts of the pull request so you can move naturally between the guide and the underlying code. If you’d prefer to disable this feature, you can disable the Generate Pull Request guides toggle within your GitHub integration settings' Pull Requests section.  Diff view options Unified and split view Linear supports both Unified and Split diff views when reviewing a pull request. Unified shows changes in a single column, while Split shows the before and after side-by-side to make comparisons easier. You can switch between Unified and Split from the pull request display options, and you can also toggle between Split and Unified view with Ctrl or ⌘ + B.  Note that on smaller screens, Split view may be unavailable if there isn't enough horizontal space. Stacked PRs  Stacked PRs let you split a larger change into smaller, dependent pull requests, with each building on the one before it. Linear displays the stack’s order and your current position, so you can move between pull requests while reviewing. Each member’s review, check, and merge status helps you understand what’s ready and what still needs attention.\\n\\nLinear supports GitHub-native stacks and linked pull requests inferred from their branch relationships. For native stacks, merging a pull request also merges every pull request below it, leaving those above it open. Linear shows how many pull requests the action affects and tracks GitHub’s asynchronous merge progress. Linked branch chains merge individually, not as a single stack. \\nStacks are created and managed outside Linear; Linear doesn’t create, reorder, rebase, or restack them, however you can ask your agent to use stacks. Structural highlighting When structural highlight is disabled, the diff uses a standard line-based comparison, so added and removed lines are emphasized. When it’s on, Linear uses syntax-aware highlighting to better understand the structure of the code and highlight the specific parts of a line that changed, such as renamed variables, edited expressions, or moved code blocks. Open GitHub PR URLs in Linear If you have an existing GitHub pull request URL, you can also open that same pull request in Linear by replacing github.com in your URL with linear.review.  Original Github URL: github.com/owner/repo/pull/123 New Linear URL: linear.review/owner/repo/pull/123 With this pattern, Linear will redirect you to the matching pull request review page in your Linear workspace automatically. Organize files with .gitattributes Use .gitattributes to organize files in pull request diffs by review category. This helps reviewers focus on implementation changes while keeping tests, documentation, generated files, and other supporting changes easy to find.  Add rules to your repository’s .gitattributes file: tests/** review-test\\ndocs/** review-documentation\\ngenerated/** linguist-generated=true\\n*.stories.tsx -review-test Use .gitattributes to group pull request files by type. Linear supports review-implementation, review-test, review-documentation, review-generated, review-agent-guidance, review-localization, and review-assets. GitHub-compatible linguist-generated and linguist-documentation work too. Add a category with attribute or attribute=true; remove one with -attribute or attribute=false. In a pull request, choose File type grouping to browse the resulting categories. Pull request preview links If your PR contains one or more preview links, this will add a preview link shortcut to the Linear issue. More details on this feature here. Notifications With Diffs enabled, Linear can notify you about pull request activity in your inbox and push notifications.  In  Code & reviews settings, you can choose which kinds of review activity reach you.  Comments & reviews covers comments, mentions, and submitted reviews, and you can choose whether to include all activity or exclude activity from bot actors. Review requests controls requests for your personal review.  Team review requests controls review requests that come through your GitHub teams, which apply when a pull request is assigned to a GitHub team you belong to rather than directly to you. Note that team review requests are only sent for GitHub teams with 10 or fewer members. Checks & merge queue controls notifications about failed checks and merge queue updates.  Additional settings Navigate into Code & reviews settings for additional preferences to the Reviews experience within Linear. These settings are personal, so they only affect your own workflow. Auto-convert draft PRs When enabling the Auto-convert draft PRs toggle, this setting automatically moves a draft pull request into a ready-for-review status once a review is requested or when the pull request is approved.  Code theme You can control the appearance of code while manually reviewing the diff or following a guided review. This includes separate themes for light and dark mode, along with display settings that provide control over the font size and line height. Move issues to start Save yourself a few steps by toggling on the automation that moves a Linear issue into a started status when you copy the git branch name. To set up this automation, refer to the On git branch copy, move issues to a started status toggle in your Code & reviews settings. Extend this further by enabling automation within your Preferences that assigns the issues you move to a started status to yourself.  Review Platform On behalf of attribution Pull request activity can show when a comment was made by an AI coding agent on behalf of a person. This attribution makes it clear which agent produced the comment and, when available, the model it used. You’ll see the agent’s name and avatar alongside the comment, helping reviewers understand the source of the feedback or update. Supported agents include Claude Code, Codex, Linear, Pi, and OpenCode. On behalf of attribution example <!-- linear:extension {\\n  \\\"version\\\": 1,\\n  \\\"plugins\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"plugin\\\": \\\"onBehalfOf\\\",\\n      \\\"agent\\\": \\\"codex\\\",\\n      \\\"model\\\": \\\"gpt-5\\\"\\n    }\\n  ]\\n} --> agent is required. Supported values are claude, codex, linear, pi, and opencode; other values are shown as a generic agent. model is optional and can be up to 200 characters. Multiple plugins can be included in the same block. Extension-only comments are hidden from Linear by default. Add \\\"visible\\\":true to preserve the GitHub comment in the activity feed. On-behalf-of blocks are visible by default unless \\\"visible\\\":false is set. Risk score Risk scores help reviewers quickly assess the potential impact of a pull request. When a connected provider supplies a score, Linear displays one of four levels: Low risk, Typical risk, High risk, Very high risk The score may also include an explanation, along with who reported it and when it was last updated. Use this context to prioritize review attention, it complements, rather than replaces, your team’s normal review process. Risk score example <!-- linear:extension {\\n  \\\"version\\\": 1,\\n  \\\"plugins\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"plugin\\\": \\\"riskScore\\\",\\n      \\\"sha\\\": \\\"0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef01234567\\\",\\n      \\\"level\\\": 3,\\n      \\\"explanations\\\": [\\n        \\\"Touches authentication\\\",\\n        \\\"Changes database migrations\\\"\\n      ]\\n    }\\n  ]\\n} --> level is required and must be an integer from 1 (low risk) to 4 (very high risk). sha is optional and must be a 40-character commit SHA. Omit it to apply the score to the pull request’s current revision. explanations is optional: up to 8 non-empty strings, each up to 200 characters. Add data from GitHub Connected tools can add this metadata in an HTML comment on a GitHub pull request, review, or review comment. Use a linear:extension block with version 1 and one or more plugins: <!-- linear:extension {\\\"version\\\":1,\\\"plugins\\\":[...]} --> FAQ AI Privacy Linear does not utilize your data to train its own AI models. Any data processed to enable Linear's AI features is shared with our trusted partners (AI subprocessors, see our DPA) exclusively to deliver those AI functionalities to you without permission to train on provided data. To provide features powered by AI and large language models (LLMs), Linear utilizes voluntary data provided by the user in terms of labeling feature outputs (thumbs up/down) or in other opt-in ways. If you have any questions or concerns, please let us know at security@linear.app. For further information, please see AI Security FAQ in our Trust Center. How do I update a PR that is showing the wrong state? This can happen sometimes if a webhook is missed between Linear and GitHub and we fail to capture the change in PR state automatically. \\n\\nYou can trigger an update to the PR state shown in Linear by making a small edit to the PR description in GitHub — for example adding and removing a space. Why didn't someone get notified on GitHub when I mentioned them from Linear? GitHub only recognizes mentions for users it can map to a GitHub identity. If you mention someone in Linear who hasn't connected their personal GitHub account (or otherwise isn't mapped), GitHub won't treat it as a real @mention — so they won't get a GitHub notification. The fix is for that person to connect GitHub in Linear so mentions can resolve correctly. Can I view and review a pull request commit by commit in Linear? Yes. You can view a pull request’s changes commit by commit by selecting individual commits to filter on in the diff. Reviews are still submitted at the pull request level rather than as separate per-commit reviews, and you can comment on specific lines or changes. Why can’t I always create or view inline comments in a diff? With GitHub’s current API, some inline comments can’t always be shown in Linear or created from Linear in the same way they appear in GitHub.  We’re tracking improvements to make this experience more consistent. Do you show check annotations or rich external status details (like inline CI annotations)? Not yet. Linear shows overall check status and basic details, but doesn't currently sync or display rich check-run annotations (for example, inline failure locations or detailed external tool output). Why don't draft/unfinished reviews sync between GitHub and Linear? Draft reviews that are started in GitHub but not submitted don't sync into Linear today. Linear syncs review state when a review is actually submitted, and it doesn't mirror GitHub's draft review state due to API limitations.\"},{\"id\":\"a29ea886-1f73-4682-ae13-726e37a4ecb0\",\"title\":\"Loops\",\"url\":\"https://linear.app/docs/loops\",\"snippet\":\"Available on paid plans. Loops that use Linear Agent draw from your workspace’s AI credits. Overview Loops use Linear Agent to drive your work forward automatically. Loops run on a schedule or in response to supported events on issues, projects, initiatives, cycles, releases, and pull requests. They can also run when a new message is posted in a selected public Slack channel. Use loops to automate routine tasks and free up your attention. You can send weekly digests of your team’s work to Slack, populate project documents with customer feedback from customer requests, or direct Linear to open a coding session when well-scoped bugs hit Triage.  Loops work in your Slack workspace through Linear’s Slack integration, and you can extend their capabilities by adding other optional MCP connectors. Example loops Here are some basic loops that are useful for most workspaces. Create custom loops for your team by prompting Linear, or get started with one of these examples. Investigate and delegate bug reports When new bugs arrive in Triage, check the codebase for a root cause. Delegate the issue to Linear to fix if the cause is found. (Build this loop ↗) Summarize weekly accomplishments Every week, summarize the week's accomplishments for a person or team and share the highlights. (Build this loop ↗) Send the pre-filled request to start setting up this custom loop. Create platform-specific versions of incoming requests When a new incoming issue is created, determine if new issues also need to be created for multiple platforms. (Build this loop ↗) Create user-facing messaging after issues close After an issue closes, check the PR that closed it and generate user-facing messaging that Support can share with customers. (Build this loop ↗) How Linear uses loops Teams at Linear use loops to automate work. Learn more about some of our own use cases: Slow PostgreSQL queries are surfaced for review Technical sales questions get automatically answered in Slack  Customer feedback is routinely collated for Product When help documentation falls out of date, a loop suggests changes Create loops with Linear Agent The best approach is to first achieve the outcome you want in a Linear Agent chat, then refine it into a loop. For example, you might be leading a project that has check-in meetings every Thursday morning. You're about to write the agenda, but realize you could automate this instead. Press ⌘/Ctrl + J to open a Linear Agent chat Give Linear Agent a prompt such as: Review the project’s Slack channel, the open questions in the latest project update, and any relevant issue threads. Add Open questions, Relevant issue threads, and Next steps sections to the Meetings project document. Summarize your findings under each heading and include links to the relevant messages or issues. Review the result to make sure it looks right. If it doesn't, continue prompting Linear until the agenda looks complete, and in the desired format. Then, ask Linear to create the loop. You’ll be prompted to add any missing details. Create loops manually Create a loop from a template, describe what you want Linear to automate, or start from scratch. Open Loops in the workspace sidebar, or open a team and select its Loops tab Select New loop Choose a template, enter a prompt, or select Start from scratch Choose where the loop belongs if prompted To configure a loop from scratch: Choose a trigger and configure its schedule, conditions, repositories, or Slack channels Add instructions describing the outcome you want Add any required connectors Under Capabilities & access, review the loop’s scope, reasoning level, and permissions Select Create loop Create from a prompt In the new loop dialog, describe what you want to automate. Linear prepares a draft with instructions and a suggested trigger. Review the draft, adjust its configuration, and select Create loop when it is ready. Start from a template Select a template to start with a predefined workflow. Answer Linear’s setup questions to adapt it to your workspace, and review the instructions, trigger, and permissions as part of setup. Workspace templates Save a configured loop as a workspace template to reuse its workflow when creating other loops. Open an eligible loop Open the more menu and select Save as template Set a name, icon, and optional description Select Save template To use a workspace template, open New loop and select its card. Linear creates a separate draft and helps adapt it to the destination. Review the configuration and permissions before creating the loop. Templates reuse instructions, supported trigger configuration, schedules, and compatible settings. They do not copy the source loop’s owner, enabled state, team access, trusted external sources, or connector authorizations. Review references in the instructions and configure the access and connections required by the new loop. Only eligible loops can be saved as templates. Slack/chat, pull request, and webhook triggers are not supported. Triage templates require a destination team with triage enabled. Workspace owners can control who manages workspace templates in Settings → Security → Workspace management → Manage workspace templates. Saving a loop as a template also requires permission to edit that loop. To manage a saved template, open the more menu on its card in the new loop chooser. Select Edit details to change its name, icon, or description, or Delete to remove it. Edit details does not change the saved workflow configuration. To save different instructions or settings, create another template from a configured loop. Pull request triggers Pull request triggers run a loop when selected activity occurs in connected GitHub repositories. Pull request triggers require a connected GitHub repository that your workspace allows loops to access. Enable code-reading access for the loop and select at least one repository. Personal GitHub access alone does not grant a loop access to a repository. In the loop editor, open Trigger → Pull request and choose an event Select the repositories to monitor Add filters to narrow the pull requests that can trigger the loop Add instructions and review the loop’s access before creating it Supported events include pull request creation, ready for review, status changes, reviewers added or removed, GitHub team reviewers added or removed, GitHub labels added or removed, commits pushed, checks passed or failed, reviews submitted, new comments, and resolved comments. To run a loop after a pull request is merged, choose Trigger → Pull request → Status → Merged. The loop runs when a pull request in the selected repositories changes to Merged and matches its filters. Merged is a pull request status transition, not a deployment event. Later updates to an already merged pull request do not re-trigger it, and checks passing does not mean every individual successful check produces a run. For example, run a loop when the GitHub label QA Test is added, and have it review the pull request’s changes and draft a QA checklist. Choose Trigger → Pull request → Labels → Added, then select QA Test. This matches the label being added. A separate Labels filter checks which labels are on the pull request after the change—use it to narrow the workflow further, such as requiring a mobile label. For a removal trigger, select the label under Labels → Removed. Don’t also require that same label in a Labels filter, because it is no longer on the pull request. If the required repository is unavailable, ask a workspace admin to configure the GitHub integration and repository code access. Workspace repository access for loops is controlled in Settings → Loops → Allow loops to access code. In the loop editor, review Capabilities & access → Access code and allow Read or Read & write as appropriate. Loops that use a pull request trigger cannot be saved as workspace templates. Webhook triggers Run a loop when an external service sends a webhook to its URL. Use webhook triggers to connect monitoring alerts, application events, or other external workflows to Linear. Go to Loops → New loop → Start from scratch. Under Trigger, select Webhook. Copy the webhook URL and secret. Use Copy example request for a ready-to-use request. Add instructions describing how the loop should interpret the payload and what actions to take, then review its access and create the loop. Send an HTTP POST request to the webhook URL with these headers: Authorization: Bearer <secret>\\nContent-Type: application/json\\nWebhook-Id: <unique-event-id> Include the event details in the request body: {\\n  \\\"event\\\": \\\"order.created\\\",\\n  \\\"orderId\\\": \\\"1042\\\"\\n} The loop receives the request body as context automatically. Include any information it needs to carry out your instructions. Use a unique Webhook-Id for each event and reuse that ID when retrying the same event with the same secret to avoid duplicate runs. A successful request returns 202 Accepted; this confirms receipt, not completion of the loop. Webhook triggers also support Standard Webhooks signatures as an alternative to providing the secret via the Authentication header. Manage secrets through Webhook details…. Keep secrets private; rotating or removing a secret invalidates its previous value immediately. Update loops Open the loop and make your changes. Select Save in the bar at the bottom of the editor. Only people with permission to edit the loop can save changes. Review a loop's runs Each loop has a run history to let you audit its behavior. Open a loop’s Run history to ask Linear Agent follow-up questions or request additional actions using the run’s context. Open Loops from the workspace or team that contains the loop Open the loop you want to review Select Run history Review recent runs to see when the loop executed and what actions it took Restore a previous version All published versions of a loop are saved and can be restored. Removed connectors must be added and authenticated again. Open the loop for editing Select Published versions Choose the version to restore Select Restore version Slack message triggers Run a loop when a new top-level message is posted in selected public Slack channels. In the loop editor, choose Trigger → Slack → Message posted Select the Slack workspace and channels to monitor Add instructions describing when the loop should act and what it should do Review its connectors and Capabilities & access, then create the loop A workspace admin must turn on Enable Linear Agent and Allow Loops access in Settings → Integrations → Slack → workspace. You must also connect your Slack account and be a full member of that Slack workspace. Slack message triggers do not support externally shared channels, private channels, direct messages, thread replies, or message edits. Messages from guests, posts from Linear or Linear Asks, and messages mentioning Linear’s bots do not trigger runs. Message-history access must be enabled. If automatic channel joining is disabled, invite Linear to each selected channel before creating the loop. Use Slack with loops Connect a Loop to your workspace’s Slack integration to let it work in Slack as Linear. A Loop can use one connected Slack workspace. You must link your Slack account to Linear and be a full member of the workspace to connect. To connect Slack to a Loop: A workspace admin must first turn on Enable Linear Agent and Allow Loops access in Settings → Integrations → Slack → workspace In the Loop draft, open the connector menu and select Slack Select the appropriate Slack workspace The Slack integration uses your workspace’s shared Slack connection, rather than a personal Slack MCP connection. It is intended for shared automation with defined public-channel access. You should use a personal Slack MCP connector when a Loop needs access based on an individual’s Slack authorization or broader Slack tools. What loops can do in Slack Loops can find public channels by name, including Slack Connect channels, and read their message history. They can also read messages from Slack links, post new messages, and add or remove emoji reactions. Messages posted to Slack by a loop are identifiable by a direct link to its run. Loops can send direct messages to full members in Slack, and those messages are one-way In a public-channel thread started by a Loop, anyone with access to the loop run can reply from there to continue the conversation Loops can send broadcast mentions such as @here and @channel in Slack messages Slack access and limitations Loops cannot access private channels, direct-message history, or group direct messages Loops cannot search messages across the workspace, create channels, edit or delete messages, upload files, or manage Slack canvas documents Replies in Slack Connect channels, direct messages, or unrelated threads do not continue the loop conversation When automatic channel joining is disabled in Slack, Loops can only use public channels that Linear has already joined. If message history is unavailable, Loops can send messages into those channels but cannot read their history, or continue conversations through replies. MCP connectors MCP connectors extend what Linear Agent can do during a loop run. When a connected MCP is available to a loop, Linear can use it to gather context or take supported actions in other services. For example: Search or retrieve content from connected sources such as GitHub, Notion, or Sentry Post comments or updates to external services like Slack Fetch documentation, pull request details, or error reports to enrich an issue An MCP connector can only access data and perform actions that are permitted by the available MCP’s configuration and the loop's data scope. When a user connects an MCP to a loop, they authorize that loop to act in the other service, so connector access should be granted thoughtfully. Managing available Connectors Owners (Enterprise) or workspace Admins (Business) can elect to let users connect any MCP connector, or only allowlisted servers. When access is limited to Selected connectors, a workspace admin must add a connector to the allowlist before it can be used in a loop. Workspace admins and owners can configure the full set of MCP connectors allowed in a workspace in Security settings. Once an MCP server has been added, users can add it when creating or updating a loop. Adding one directly to a loop requires the configuring user to authenticate with the connector, and it will use their personal account's connection. MCP approvals To choose which tools a loop can use, edit the loop, open the MCP connector’s ⋯ menu, and select Manage tools…. Select the tools you want to make available to the loop. Enable Automatically approve all tool calls to skip approval requests from that connector. To automatically approve individual tools instead, leave this setting off and enable Always approved for each tool you want to approve automatically. Tool selection controls which tools the loop can use. Automatic approval controls whether tool calls require approval; it does not grant additional access in the connected service. Enable automatic approval only for tools you trust the loop to use without individual review. Setup Workspace owners can manage who can create and manage workspace loops in Settings → Security → Workspace management → Manage loops Workspace owners can manage who can save and manage workspace templates in Settings → Security → Workspace management → Manage workspace templates Team owners can control who can create and manage loops in Settings → Teams → [Team name] → Access and permissions → Loop management To change who can edit a specific loop, open it and then select Who can edit. Choose between all members, workspace admins or team owners, or only the loop owner. Capabilities & access Configure the loop’s scope, reasoning level, and permissions under Capabilities & access. Enable only the access it needs, and consider the impact before allowing changes or connecting external services. Team access Controls which teams the loop can access. It can read and write data only in those teams. By default, a loop at the workspace level or in a public team will have access to all public teams, while a loop inside a private team will have access only to that team. Loops that can access all public teams can also access workspace-level objects like Initiatives and Customers. Reasoning level Choose how deeply the loop should analyze information under Capabilities & access → Reasoning level. Auto lets Linear select the reasoning level Low prioritizes speed and lower cost for simple tasks Medium balances speed and depth for everyday tasks High provides more thorough reasoning for complex tasks and costs more Web access If enabled, the loop can query any website. You can use this to set up loops that can take actions like researching competitor announcements, or checking the documentation of services your product integrates with. Use caution when enabling web access. Web access can send workspace content to external services. Do not enable it for loops that may process sensitive data unless you are comfortable with that data leaving Linear. Access code Under Capabilities & access → Access code, choose how the loop can use configured repositories: Disabled prevents code access Read allows the loop to inspect code and pull requests Read & write also allows the loop to make changes through coding sessions A workspace admin must configure repository access in Settings → Loops → Allow loops to access code. Coding sessions must also be enabled to use Read & write. Externally synced issues and comments If enabled, the loop will be able to write data on issues or comment threads that are synced with external applications, like issues created from Slack with bi-directionally synced threads. Enable this with care, as some synced threads may be visible outside your workspace - if you use GitHub sync in a public repo for instance, the loop would be able to post comments to that repo's GitHub Issues. External sources Issues can be created from sources outside Linear, such as Slack messages or emails received at a particular address. By default, issue-triggered loops run only on issues created within Linear. To run an issue-triggered loop on issues created from external sources, enable the relevant sources for that loop. Workspace owners can configure the allowed external sources in Security settings, then enable them for individual loops. Slack message triggers are configured separately. Allow changes outside of triggering entity If enabled, the loop can write data to other entities within its Team access scope. If disabled, it can change only the entity that triggered the run. This setting does not apply to scheduled or Slack-triggered loops. AI credits Loops use AI credits, which workspace admins can purchase and manage in Settings. Purchased credits expire 12 months after the purchase date. If a workspace runs out of credits, Loops pause unless an admin purchases more credits or enables auto top-ups. Workspaces are charged automatically only when auto top-ups are enabled. Promotional Loops AI credits Some workspaces may receive promotional Loops AI credits with separate expiration terms. Eligible workspaces will be notified by email. FAQ What are the best practices for writing instructions? Clearly describe the intended outcome and any actions Linear Agent should avoid. For example: Investigate the issue using the available context. Add a comment summarizing the likely cause and recommend the next action. Do not change the issue's assignee or status. For best results: Use Linear Agent chat to help build your instructions. See Create loops with Linear Agent for guidance. Describe the outcome, not only an action Specify which context or connected tools Linear Agent should use State which changes are permitted What MCP connectors can loops use? A loop can only use MCP connectors that are connected to your workspace, enabled for Linear Agent, and allowed by your workspace admins. Access also depends on the loop's scope and permissions. When should web access be enabled? Enable web access only when a loop needs information from external websites. A good fit is research on public information, like competitor announcements, documentation, or API references, where the loop does not need to send sensitive workspace content to external services. Enable this intentionally, since allowing external web access can increase the risk of exposing workspace data outside Linear. How do I use Code Intelligence? A workspace admin must configure repository access in Settings → Loops → Allow loops to access code. In the loop editor, select Capabilities & access → Access code → Read. Read & write also includes code-reading access. How do I use Coding Sessions? A workspace admin must enable coding sessions and allow the required repositories in Settings → Loops → Allow loops to access code. In the loop editor, select Capabilities & access → Access code → Read & write. How can I review loop costs and AI credit usage? Open Settings → Usage & limits to review AI credit usage. Loop spend includes the loop’s own work and any coding sessions it starts. Learn more about usage and spend limits. If your workspace runs out of AI credits, loops stop running until more credits are added. Why did I get a loop run failed notification? To troubleshoot, open the loop's Run history to check recent runs and any visible failures. Common causes a loop run might fail include missing permissions, untrusted external sources, or your workspace running out of AI credits. To get a more detailed description of what went wrong, you can also ask Linear. Press ⌘/Ctrl + J while looking at the run history and ask for a detailed description of why the loop run failed. Can I run a loop manually? You can use Run now for enabled loops that use Linear Agent and have a schedule, issue, project, or initiative trigger. For issue, project, and initiative triggers, choose the entity you want to use for the run. Run now is not available for cycle triggers. Run now is not available for pull request triggers. These loops run when a matching pull request event occurs. What schedules can loops use? Scheduled loops can run hourly, daily, weekly, monthly, or yearly. Choose the timezone and when the loop should first run. Weekly schedules can run on one or more days of the week. The first run date cannot be in the past. How do I enable or disable a loop? Right-click on the loop from either the workspace or team loop view and select Enable or Disable. How do triage loops work? Triage loops are created for a specific team and require triage to be enabled for that team. They run when an issue enters the team's triage queue and begins matching the configured conditions. Triage loops do not support schedules or non-issue triggers. Are there limits on loops? Teams can create up to 100 loops, and workspaces can create up to 500 workspace-level loops. Each person can keep up to 100 unpublished loop drafts. Loop names can be up to 64 characters, and descriptions can be up to 255 characters. A loop can include up to eight conditions. What happens when I delete a loop? Deleting a loop is permanent and cannot be undone. Consider disabling the loop instead if you may need to restore it in the future. When does a loop with configured conditions run? A loop triggered when conditions start matching runs when an item begins matching those conditions. Further updates do not trigger another run while the same conditions remain matched. Event-triggered loops run whenever the selected event occurs and any configured filters match. For example, adding the QA Test label can trigger a run even if other labels on the pull request have not changed.\"},{\"id\":\"cbc3383a-de60-44c4-9b38-a7992219277b\",\"title\":\"Coding settings\",\"url\":\"https://linear.app/docs/code-and-reviews\",\"snippet\":\"Review GitHub pull requests in Linear and set your preferences for how Linear works with your git workflow. Open Settings → Account → Code & reviews to configure these preferences. Setup Enable code reviews Code reviews require a workspace GitHub integration with repository code access, a connected personal GitHub account, and Reviews enabled in Settings → Account → Code & reviews.  Workspace integration and code-access changes require workspace permissions, but each user manages their own GitHub connection individually.  See the Reviews setup guide for detailed setup instructions. Pull request preferences Auto-convert draft pull requests Automatically mark your draft pull requests as ready when a review is requested or the pull request is approved. It applies only to your own draft pull requests. Merge strategy Choose how you merge pull requests from Linear: Squash and merge (default) Rebase and merge Create merge commit Choose for each pull request This is a personal preference. Repository configuration still determines which merge methods are available, so a method disabled in GitHub, or restricted by a merge queue, cannot be used even when it is selected here. Code theme Select the syntax highlighting theme and typography used in code diffs and viewers. Make code easier to read in the way that works best for you. Choose separate syntax themes for light and dark mode, including Linear Colorblind. This lets you tailor code review views to your display mode and accessibility preferences. Some themes support additional display options. Use theme background applies the theme’s own background colors, while Show diagonal lines adds patterns to additions and removals so changes remain distinguishable without relying on color alone. These options only appear for compatible themes. Review notifications These controls determine which code review events reach your Linear inbox and your push notifications. Each one is set separately. Comments & reviews: choose All, Exclude Bots to skip comments and reviews left by bots, or None. Review requests: get notified when your review is requested. GitHub team review requests: get notified when a review is requested from a GitHub team you belong to. This requires Review requests to be enabled and applies only to GitHub teams with 10 or fewer members. Checks & merge queue: get notified when checks fail or when a pull request is removed from the merge queue. Signed commits When Require signed commits is enabled, commits made by coding sessions must be signed. This is a workspace-wide setting and applies to commits created by coding sessions, not to every commit pushed to GitHub. Changing it requires workspace admin (or owner if on Enterprise) permissions. Commit signing key is a personal setting on your own account. Add a signing key so that commits Linear makes on your behalf can be signed. It is relevant when you use coding sessions and code reviews in Linear: without a key, coding sessions cannot commit in a workspace that requires signed commits. Follow the instructions shown in the setting to generate a key and add it to your account. External tools Configure coding tools Choose the coding tools you can open Linear issues in, such as Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, and more. Terminal-based tools require the Linear desktop app on a supported platform, because Linear needs to launch a local terminal. On macOS you can also pick your default terminal application; that selector is macOS only. You can also add custom links to open issues in any tool that accepts a URL, and custom scripts to run a local command with issue context. See coding tools and open issues with custom scripts for the full workflows. Git attachment format Choose how GitHub and GitLab attachments are displayed on issues: Title, or Title+Repository. Move issue to started status Linear can move an issue to your team's first started status when you begin work on it. There are separate settings for copying the git branch name and for opening the issue in a coding tool, which includes Copy as prompt. In both cases: Issues that have already started, and closed issues, are not moved. If the issue is unassigned, it may also be assigned to you when your separate self-assignment preference is enabled. Hold Option on macOS, or Alt on Windows, while performing the action to skip the status change for that one action. Open GitHub pull requests in Choose whether pull requests open in GitHub or Graphite. This control appears once GitHub is connected and is separate from enabling reviews in Linear: it sets your external destination whether or not you review pull requests in Linear.\"}]"}],"mcpMeta":{"_meta":{"io.modelcontextprotocol/serverInfo":{"name":"linear","title":"Linear","icons":[{"src":"https://static.linear.app/integrations/mcp/icon.svg","mimeType":"image/svg+xml","sizes":["any"]},{"src":"https://static.linear.app/integrations/mcp/icon-256.png","mimeType":"image/png","sizes":["256x256"]}],"version":"1.0.2","websiteUrl":"https://linear.app","description":"Find and manage issues, projects, and documents in your Linear workspace."}}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ce445ffb-dba5-4884-894e-ba3ba07af52d","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"C:\\Users\\lcvle\\lcv-workspace","sessionId":"3815f8b0-8c60-4890-93a0-2fcc4c634f88","version":"2.1.314","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"glistening-tumbling-prism"}
{"parentUuid":"35f2ef5a-3de4-4c5d-b7bb-449668fbdcca","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-mythos-5-1","id":"msg_011CgY3BSJjJ3W9SqtywjSHb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01AAuKDAFdHrHiKBLCTtFTtg","name":"mcp__claude_ai_Linear__save_issue","input":{"id":"LCV-468","patch":[{"op":"insert_before","anchor":"## Estado vigente — 22/10/2026, 17:00 (horário de Brasília; responsável: Claude)","text":"## Estado vigente — 30/10/2026 (responsável: Claude; Codex ativo de novo)\n\n### Responsáveis\n\n1. O Codex voltou a operar em 26/10/2026 (web do Oráculo em ASTROLO-37, alertas do SCC em LCV-448, correções Guava e cartão dos anjos). A ordem de 22/10 (\"tudo com o Claude\") deixou de valer na prática para a web; a tabela \"Divisão de assuntos\" volta a ser a referência: **web = Codex**, **backend comum e Android = Claude**, conforme comentário c801013c de 26/10 e LCV-464.\n2. A coordenação da biblioteca universal (ledger e biblioteca únicos no `astrologo-paid`) está na LCV-464; esta issue continua sendo só o registro de quem cuida de quê e das convenções.\n\n### Entregas desde 22/10 (resumo; detalhe nas issues de assunto)\n\n1. **Backend comum em produção** (`astrologo-paid-00072-juv` e `astrologo-paid-internal-00059-juy`, 30/10): ledger único, biblioteca de 6 meses, Mercado Pago na web, cascata Gemini → Anthropic → OpenAI, reembolso automático, observabilidade nativa (7 métricas de log, 11 alertas). LCV-464.\n2. **Primeira compra real pela web entregue de ponta a ponta e recuperada no Android** em 30/10/2026 (ASTANDR-11/#47, ASTANDR-28/#123, ASTANDR-27/#119, ASTROLO-37/#477).\n3. **Oráculo Celestial Android 1.0.0 (21)**, primeira versão estável: PR #170 mesclada, release em revisão na faixa de produção da Play (04:05 de 30/10), ficha e capturas novas. Decisão do operador: lançamento a 100%.\n4. **Encerradas em 30/10/2026 por decisão do operador:** ASTANDR-38 (OpenTelemetry 1.67.0), ASTANDR-30 (CMEK), ASTANDR-22 (Argon principal, provado pela entrega real) e ASTANDR-20 (opção C, encerrada como está; o motor temático produz a análise).\n\n### Condição de término desta coordenação (atualizada)\n\nA cobertura editorial da ASTANDR-20 deixou de ser condição (issue encerrada em 30/10). Resta a prova real de compra pelo canal Play (ASTANDR-11/#47), que o operador fará no próprio aparelho. Quando ela sair, esta issue pode ser concluída, com a tabela de divisão preservada como referência para a web e o iOS.\n\nO bloco abaixo (22/10) é histórico.\n\n"}]},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":23076,"cache_read_input_tokens":838843,"output_tokens":1447,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":156},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":23076,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":32,"output_tokens":1447,"cache_read_input_tokens":838843,"cache_creation_input_tokens":23076,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":23076},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01AAuKDAFdHrHiKBLCTtFTtg":{"id":"LCV-468","patch":[{"op":"insert_before","anchor":"## Estado vigente — 22/10/2026, 17:00 (horário de Brasília; responsável: Claude)","text":"## Estado vigente — 30/10/2026 (responsável: Claude; Codex ativo de novo)\n\n### Responsáveis\n\n1. O Codex voltou a operar em 26/10/2026 (web do Oráculo em ASTROLO-37, alertas do SCC em LCV-448, correções Guava e cartão dos anjos). A ordem de 22/10 (\"tudo com o Claude\") deixou de valer na prática para a web; a tabela \"Divisão de assuntos\" volta a ser a referência: **web = Codex**, **backend comum e Android = Claude**, conforme comentário c801013c de 26/10 e LCV-464.\n2. A coordenação da biblioteca universal (ledger e biblioteca únicos no `astrologo-paid`) está na LCV-464; esta issue continua sendo só o registro de quem cuida de quê e das convenções.\n\n### Entregas desde 22/10 (resumo; detalhe nas issues de assunto)\n\n1. **Backend comum em produção** (`astrologo-paid-00072-juv` e `astrologo-paid-internal-00059-juy`, 30/10): ledger único, biblioteca de 6 meses, Mercado Pago na web, cascata Gemini → Anthropic → OpenAI, reembolso automático, observabilidade nativa (7 métricas de log, 11 alertas). LCV-464.\n2. **Primeira compra real pela web entregue de ponta a ponta e recuperada no Android** em 30/10/2026 (ASTANDR-11/#47, ASTANDR-28/#123, ASTANDR-27/#119, ASTROLO-37/#477).\n3. **Oráculo Celestial Android 1.0.0 (21)**, primeira versão estável: PR #170 mesclada, release em revisão na faixa de produção da Play (04:05 de 30/10), ficha e capturas novas. Decisão do operador: lançamento a 100%.\n4. **Encerradas em 30/10/2026 por decisão do operador:** ASTANDR-38 (OpenTelemetry 1.67.0), ASTANDR-30 (CMEK), ASTANDR-22 (Argon principal, provado pela entrega real) e ASTANDR-20 (opção C, encerrada como está; o motor temático produz a análise).\n\n### Condição de término desta coordenação (atualizada)\n\nA cobertura editorial da ASTANDR-20 deixou de ser condição (issue encerrada em 30/10). Resta a prova real de compra pelo canal Play (ASTANDR-11/#47), que o operador fará no próprio aparelho. Quando ela sair, esta issue pode ser concluída, com a tabela de divisão preservada como referência para a web e o iOS.\n\nO bloco abaixo (22/10) é histórico.\n\n"}]}},"apiBlockIndex":3,"requestId":"req_011CgY3BRsSbqME1UcbXSBky","requestRef":"ef9e2770-1963-47a4-b93a-c330e7c2fc8e","requestedModel":"claude-mythos-5-1","attributionMcpServer":"claude.ai Linear","attributionMcpTool":"save_issue","type":"assistant","uuid":"60ad6300-bef8-4836-8893-29f6c3ec96b1","timestamp":"2026-10-30T07:37:16.020Z","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":null,"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"C:\\Users\\lcvle\\lcv-workspace","sessionId":"3815f8b0-8c60-4890-93a0-2fcc4c634f88","version":"2.1.314","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"glistening-tumbling-prism"}
{"type":"last-prompt","lastPrompt":"LCV-468 - atualize o que for nmecessário atualizar.; LCV-464 - alguém usou o víncul oerrado e o PR fecheou. Há três tiposd e vínculo no Linear (veja na documentação oficial); ASTANDR-38 e ASTANDR-30 -…","leafUuid":"60ad6300-bef8-4836-8893-29f6c3ec96b1","sessionId":"3815f8b0-8c60-4890-93a0-2fcc4c634f88"}
