{"parentUuid":"6e1822f5-7b18-4432-9495-8c3f6fb9df79","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"110dcd88-4da4-456d-b567-dca3461d0c76","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CrEoZWJXNX1uNuHSvn7kzY","type":"tool_result","content":"== GitLab https://linear.app/docs/gitlab\r\n  [magic] u can use to create a new branch in GitLab. We recommend using common branch naming patterns throughout all of your teams. Add the issue ID in the MR title Include the Linear issue ID (e.g. ID-123) in the MR title when creating merge requests. Use a magic word Add a magic word + issue ID in the MR description (e.g. Fixes ENG-123). The integration cannot link MRs via comments or commit messages. The available magic words to link issues are: close, closes, closed, closing, fix, fixes, fixed, fixing, resolve, resolves, resolved, resolving, complete, completes, completed, completing, implement, im\r\n  --\r\n  [closes] the MR title when creating merge requests. Use a magic word Add a magic word + issue ID in the MR description (e.g. Fixes ENG-123). The integration cannot link MRs via comments or commit messages. The available magic words to link issues are: close, closes, closed, closing, fix, fixes, fixed, fixing, resolve, resolves, resolved, resolving, complete, completes, completed, completing, implement, implements, implemented, implementing. To link MRs to issues without them automating your issue status on MR merge, use the one of the contributing magic words: ref, references, part of, contributes to, \r\n  --\r\n  [non-closing] ing, implement, implements, implemented, implementing. To link MRs to issues without them automating your issue status on MR merge, use the one of the contributing magic words: ref, references, part of, contributes to, towards, updates. When using a non-closing magic word like this, the linked MR or commit will still move the issue through other statuses per Workflow settings, but will not automate the issue's status when the MR or commit merges. Instead, it will only transition the issue through to what’s configured prior to the On MR or commit merge event. Use related to or relates to to cre\r\n  --\r\n  [branch name] ure Enable SSL verification is checked Click Save changes. Link Merge Requests Create a new branch Set the branch format in Linear Settings > Workspace > Integrations GitLab > Branch format. When viewing or selecting a Linear issue, use the Copy git branch name action or shortcut Cmd/Ctrl + Shift + . It will give you a branch name with the issue ID which you can use to create a new branch in GitLab. We recommend using common branch naming patterns throughout all of your teams. Add the issue ID in the MR title Include the Linear issue ID (e.g. ID-123) in the MR title when creating merge request\r\n  --\r\n  [merged] Linear supports linking your Linear issues to GitLab and automates your merge request workflows. Overview Link Linear issues to GitLab merge requests. Automate your MR workflow so that issues update when MRs are drafted, opened, merged and when reviews are requested. You can link single or multiple issues to a specific MR. Configure the GitLab integration We support linking GitLab merge requests for both hosted and self-hosted installations, as long as your installation is publicly available.  Navigate to Linear Settings > Features > Integrations > GitLab Click Enable to l\r\n  --\r\n== Origin https://linear.app/docs/origin\r\n  [magic] equests to issues and teams can set up workflow automations. Linking pull requests Link a PR to a Linear issue by including the issue ID in the branch name or PR title. To link more than one PR, or to link an issue after the PR already exists, use a magic word like “fixes” followed by the issue ID in the PR description: Fixes LIN-123. Linking creates a reference to the PR on the Linear issue and a link back to Linear from the PR. Status automations Workflow automations are set per team and move issues through your workflow as their PRs progress. For example, an issue can move to In Progress wh\r\n  --\r\n  [branch name] e owner or admin can enable the Origin integration in Settings. Once it’s enabled, members can link pull requests to issues and teams can set up workflow automations. Linking pull requests Link a PR to a Linear issue by including the issue ID in the branch name or PR title. To link more than one PR, or to link an issue after the PR already exists, use a magic word like “fixes” followed by the issue ID in the PR description: Fixes LIN-123. Linking creates a reference to the PR on the Linear issue and a link back to Linear from the PR. Status automations Workflow automations are set per team and\r\n  --\r\n  [Done] on the Linear issue and a link back to Linear from the PR. Status automations Workflow automations are set per team and move issues through your workflow as their PRs progress. For example, an issue can move to In Progress when a PR is opened and to Done when it’s merged. Configure each team-specific automation by navigating into the team-level Settings > Workflows & automations. Members can also turn on personal automations in their preferences, which auto-assign the issue to them and move it to In Progress when they open a linked PR.\r\n  --\r\n  [merged] Overview Linear’s Origin integration keeps your issues and pull requests in sync. Link an Origin pull request to a Linear issue, and Linear updates the issue’s status as the PR moves from draft to review to merged, so nobody has to change statuses by hand. Every linked PR appears on its issue in Linear, with a link back to the issue from the PR in Origin. Anyone looking at either side can see where the work stands. Configure A Linear workspace owner or admin can enable the Origin integration in Settings. Once it’s enabled, members can link pull reques\r\n  --\r\n== Reviews https://linear.app/docs/diffs\r\n  [branch name] ht and dark mode, along with display settings that provide control over the font size and line height. Move issues to start Save yourself a few steps by toggling on the automation that moves a Linear issue into a started status when you copy the git branch name. To set up this automation, refer to the On git branch copy, move issues to a started status toggle in your Code & reviews settings. Extend this further by enabling automation within your Preferences that assigns the issues you move to a started status to yourself.  Review Platform On behalf of attribution Pull request activity can show\r\n  --\r\n== Loops https://linear.app/docs/loops\r\n  [closes] . Create platform-specific versions of incoming requests When a new incoming issue is created, determine if new issues also need to be created for multiple platforms. (Build this loop ↗) Create user-facing messaging after issues close After an issue closes, check the PR that closed it and generate user-facing messaging that Support can share with customers. (Build this loop ↗) How Linear uses loops Teams at Linear use loops to automate work. Learn more about some of our own use cases: Slow PostgreSQL queries are surfaced for review Technical sales questions get automatically answered in Slack \r\n  --\r\n  [merged] tatus changes, reviewers added or removed, GitHub team reviewers added or removed, GitHub labels added or removed, commits pushed, checks passed or failed, reviews submitted, new comments, and resolved comments. To run a loop after a pull request is merged, choose Trigger → Pull request → Status → Merged. The loop runs when a pull request in the selected repositories changes to Merged and matches its filters. Merged is a pull request status transition, not a deployment event. Later updates to an already merged pull request do not re-trigger it, and checks passing does not mean every individual\r\n  --\r\n== Coding settings https://linear.app/docs/code-and-reviews\r\n  [branch name] w GitHub and GitLab attachments are displayed on issues: Title, or Title+Repository. Move issue to started status Linear can move an issue to your team's first started status when you begin work on it. There are separate settings for copying the git branch name and for opening the issue in a coding tool, which includes Copy as prompt. In both cases: Issues that have already started, and closed issues, are not moved. If the issue is unassigned, it may also be assigned to you when your separate self-assignment preference is enabled. Hold Option on macOS, or Alt on Windows, while performing the a\r\n  --","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"4e3db7d2-4422-40a9-85bf-49252a738d57","timestamp":"2026-10-30T07:37:26.160Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"== GitLab https://linear.app/docs/gitlab\r\n  [magic] u can use to create a new branch in GitLab. We recommend using common branch naming patterns throughout all of your teams. Add the issue ID in the MR title Include the Linear issue ID (e.g. ID-123) in the MR title when creating merge requests. Use a magic word Add a magic word + issue ID in the MR description (e.g. Fixes ENG-123). The integration cannot link MRs via comments or commit messages. The available magic words to link issues are: close, closes, closed, closing, fix, fixes, fixed, fixing, resolve, resolves, resolved, resolving, complete, completes, completed, completing, implement, im\r\n  --\r\n  [closes] the MR title when creating merge requests. Use a magic word Add a magic word + issue ID in the MR description (e.g. Fixes ENG-123). The integration cannot link MRs via comments or commit messages. The available magic words to link issues are: close, closes, closed, closing, fix, fixes, fixed, fixing, resolve, resolves, resolved, resolving, complete, completes, completed, completing, implement, implements, implemented, implementing. To link MRs to issues without them automating your issue status on MR merge, use the one of the contributing magic words: ref, references, part of, contributes to, \r\n  --\r\n  [non-closing] ing, implement, implements, implemented, implementing. To link MRs to issues w